    summary_en: "The European Telecommunications Standards Institute (ETSI) has released a new technical specification, ETSI TS 104 223, which aims to provide reliable and implementable cybersecurity guidance for Artificial Intelligence (AI) models and systems. This specification adopts a full lifecycle approach, defining 13 core principles across 5 lifecycle stages, which expands to 72 traceable principles to enhance the security of all AI systems. Due to the unique and complex cyber threats faced by AI systems—such as data poisoning, model obfuscation, and indirect prompt injection—this standard offers targeted guidance, integrating existing cybersecurity practices with novel AI security methodologies. The specification provides a robust set of foundational security requirements for all stakeholders in the AI supply chain, including developers, suppliers, integrators, and operators. Furthermore, ETSI will issue practical implementation guides for Small and Medium-sized Enterprises (SMEs), including case studies for various deployment environments, to help organizations effectively apply these security requirements. This standard is considered a global first, aiming to embed cybersecurity into every stage of the AI system's design, development, deployment, operation, and maintenance, thereby ensuring AI trustworthiness.",
